One consideration: backend APIs

Since your scanner orchestration, billing, RBAC, and enterprise workflows will likely grow beyond "lightweight APIs," I'd recommend having a dedicated backend (Express, FastAPI, or NestJS) running in your DigitalOcean Kubernetes cluster rather than relying heavily on Vercel Functions.

That gives you:

✅ Long-running API requests when needed
✅ Consistent networking to scanner services
✅ Easier service-to-service authentication
✅ Better control over scaling
✅ No serverless execution constraints

Vercel then focuses on delivering the frontend, while Kubernetes hosts your core platform services.

Overall assessment ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐ (9.8/10)

For an enterprise SaaS like QGuard Helix, I would recommend:

🌐 Cloudflare: DNS, CDN, WAF, TLS, DDoS, Zero Trust
💻 Vercel: Next.js frontend and lightweight edge/serverless functionality
🗄️ Supabase: Authentication, PostgreSQL, Realtime, Storage
☸️ DigitalOcean Kubernetes: Scanner engines, orchestration services, backend APIs, background workers
🚀 Redis (Managed): Queues, caching, distributed coordination
📦 Object Storage: Start with Supabase Storage; consider DigitalOcean Spaces later if storage volume or cost makes it advantageous

This is a robust, cloud-native architecture that separates responsibilities cleanly and should serve QGuard Helix well from MVP through enterprise-scale deployments.